  "query_preview": "..Monsoon moisture will remain over our region for the\nnext several days, with precipitable water values above the 90th\npercentile through at least Wednesday, and approaching the 99th\npercentile in eastern Arizona late Tuesday through late Wednesday.\nAs of 130 PM, most of the storm activity today has been in Navajo\nand Apache counties, with a few strong storms and some locally\nheavy rainfall/flash flooding. Drier air and a capping inversion \nhave limited storm development farther west in Yavapai/Coconino \ncounties so far and much of the hi-res guidance suggests this will\ncontinue to be the case through this evening. Later tonight \nthrough Tuesday morning - there is a chance that activity forming\nin northern Mexico and southeast Arizona could move into east \ncentral Arizona mainly east of I-17. Tuesday afternoon - looking\nactive again over the eastern half of the state, with heavy\nrainfall/flash flooding the main threat. Hi-res models show\nactivity a bit farther west that we have seen so far today, back\nto the US89/I-17 corridor. Tuesday night through Wednesday -\nmodels have been fairly consistent for a couple of days in showing\na disturbance moving northward along the Gulf of California up\ninto Arizona. This may bring a better chance of overnight storms\nlate Tuesday night/Wednesday morning, with a potential for heavy\nrainfall. THe 12Z HREF is showing a moderate signal for heavy\nrainfall early Wednesday morning south of I-40. This threat should\nspread northward through the day Wednesday and into Wednesday\nnight. Moisture remains over the region Thursday with 30-60% PoPs\ncontinuing. By the end of the week and weekend, troughing passing\nby to the north may introduce enough westerly flow and drying to\nlower storm chances to less than 20-30%.",
